A||N||4||jazer||Tranny Help||||9||
Z||000000||jazer||11-23-2000||07:22 PM||||In January, I took my 87 5-speed coupe to Mr. Transmission to do the clutch.  I did it because 1) they were cheap, 2)Big Name.  A few months later I took it in and had the GT exhaust setup put in and they found that the cradle bolts were only hand tightened.  A couple of months after that I started having trouble shifting from 1st to 2nd and it started to grind in third.  I know that it is probably a syncro causing the grinding.  I put some moly slip in and noticed that the tranny fluid was red.  I never thought anything of it until I started reading up on it.  Could they have put automatic tranny fluid instead of 5w30?  It's been in there for almost a year now.  Has it done its damage or is my tranny just going naturally?  Can it be saved?  Thanks ||||reg||9
Z||000001||theogre||11-23-2000||09:42 PM||||According to Haynes and what I've seen in other cars, some of the 5 speeds take ATF. I don't know which, if any of, the Fiero ones do. The tranny shop would have, I hope, looked it up and put in whatever was correct.<P>It's hard to say if the use of ATF in a 5 that wants 5W-30 would be damaged by it. The ATF is in roughly the same viscosity range I think. ATF is a little more seal friendly than engine oil often is. If it was going to do damage I would think it would chew bearings or gears before a syncro. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||StuGood||11-24-2000||02:04 PM||||FWIW, the owner's manual in my 1987 says to put 5W-30 in the 4-cyl 5-speed.<P>Wonder if your clutch isn't releasing fully?||||reg||1
Z||000003||PlasticWrapd||11-27-2000||10:20 PM||||Is it the Isuzu 5?  Isuzu recommends ATF in all of their 5 speed trannys.  GM switched it to 5w-30 because the ATF shears more easily (allows metal-to-metal contact).  Perhaps the tranny had ATF from the (Isuzu)factory and someone continued to replace the oil with ATF?||||reg||1
Z||000004||PlasticWrapd||11-27-2000||10:22 PM||||Another thought....  If the clutch isn't releasing properly....<P>Check the clutch pedal. They tend to twist, allowing play.  If it's twisted, take a large pair of Vice-Grips and twist it back into shape.  A twisted clutch pedal will keep the clutch from releasing fully.  ||||reg||1
